|~ ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   qlang LSP showcase

   Open this file in VS Code with the qlang extension active
   to exercise the language server:
     1. Hover — the page of a word, a path, a keyword
     2. Ctrl+Click — Go to Definition
     3. Shift+F12 — Find References
     4. Ctrl+Shift+O — Outline
     5. Inside a call — Signature Help
     6. Ctrl+Space — Completion
     7. Typing — Diagnostics

   The file is one query: each example joins the one before it by
   `|`, and its literal replaces the value in the pipe. Code a
   query names is held under a bare name and run by `apply`; a
   word, called by its name on a value, is declared under the
   label of the values it serves, and `::any` holds the words
   that mean the same for every value.
   The decisions it exists for: [QSSLF#catalog]. ~|


|~| ── 1. Hover ─────────────────────────────────────────────
|~| Hover count, /name and :age.

[1 2 3 4 5] | count

| ({:name "Ann" :age 30} | get /name)


|~| ── 2. Code ──────────────────────────────────────────────
|~| A quote, `~(…)`, is code: `apply` runs it on the value in the
|~| pipe, `map` on each element, `filter` to keep the elements it
|~| holds of. A name holds code as it holds any value, a word that
|~| takes code takes the name bare, and the name alone reads the
|~| quote itself. Ctrl+Click double to jump to its declaration;
|~| Shift+F12 finds the declaration and its uses.

| (5 | apply ~(add 1 | mul 2))

| ([1 2 3] | map ~(mul 2))

| ([1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1))

:double |~~ Doubles a number. ~~| ~(mul 2)

| (5 | apply double)

| ([1 2 3] | map double)

| (5 | double)


|~| ── 3. A kind of your own ─────────────────────────────────
|~| A kind is built from `::kind` by commands, `reads` a field of
|~| its payload held to its kinds, and a value put under it keeps
|~| them. A word declared under it serves its values alone, called
|~| by its name on the value, and is built from `::fn`, `takes` an
|~| input of the call held to its kinds.

:Person |~~ A person, a name and an age. ~~| (::kind
  | wraps |~~ The name and the age. ~~| ::map
  | reads :name |~~ The name. ~~| /name ::string
  | reads :age |~~ The age in years. ~~| /age ::number)
:Person/adult |~~ Whether the person is of age. ~~| ~(get /age | gte 18)
:Person/greet |~~ The person's name after a greeting. ~~| (::fn
  | takes :greeting |~~ The words before the name. ~~| /0 ::string
  | impl ~(get /name | prepend greeting))

| (::Person{:name "Ann" :age 30} | adult)

| (::Person{:name "Ann" :age 30} | greet "hello ")

| (::Person{:name "Ann" :age 30} | greet 1 !| type)

| (::Person{:name 7 :age 30} !| type)

| ({:name "Bo" :age 15} | adult !| type)


|~| ── 4. Two fields of one record ───────────────────────────
|~| A modifier is computed against the subject of its own step,
|~| so two fields meet in one step, or a name keeps the record
|~| for a later one.

| ({:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty)

| ({:price 4 :qty 3} | :r it | get /qty | mul (r | get /price))


|~| ── 5. A fold ─────────────────────────────────────────────
|~| A word of one name folds with the element as its modifier;
|~| code of several steps runs on the pair of the value so far
|~| and the element.

| ([1 2 3] | reduce 0 ~(add))

| ([{:x 1} {:x 2}] | reduce 0 ~(sumOf /0 (get /1 | get /x)))


|~| ── 6. Names and their scope ──────────────────────────────
|~| A name is declared once in its scope, and one declared inside
|~| parentheses closes with them.

:x 1
:y (x | add 1)
| (42 | :inner it | inner)
| y


|~| ── 7. Signature Help and Completion ──────────────────────
|~| Inside a call of a word of the language the active input
|~| follows the modifier under the cursor: condition, then,
|~| else. After `|` Ctrl+Space lists the words the kind of the
|~| value answers and the names the file declares above.

| (5 | if (gt 0) ~("pos") ~("neg"))

| ("text" | upper)


|~| ── 8. Failures ───────────────────────────────────────────
|~| `|` passes a failure by and `!|` catches it; `explain`
|~| answers the page of what refused, which tells the repair.

| ("hello" | add 1 !| type)

| ([1 "x" 3] | map ~(add 10 !| 0))

| ("hello" | add 1 !| explain)


|~| ── 9. Comments ───────────────────────────────────────────
|~| A comment, a line one or a block one, is whitespace;
|~| a doc stands in the slot of the declaration it documents.

|~ a block comment between two steps ~|
:z |~~ The doc in the slot of z. ~~| 1
